%I A399718 #22 Sep 26 2026 21:26:26
%S A399718 0,1,1,1,2,3,5,7,9,16,23,30,37,44,51,58,65,72,79,86,93,100,107,114,
%T A399718 121,128,135,142,149,156,163,170,177,184,191,198,205,212,219,226,445,
%U A399718 671,897,1123,1349,1575,1801,2027,2253,2479,2705,2931,3157,3383,3609
%N A399718 Denominators of "Farey fraction" approximations to Pi/2.
%C A399718 Given a real number x >= 1 (here x = Pi/2), start with 1/0 and 0/1 and construct the sequence of fractions f_n = r_n/s_n such that:
%C A399718 f_{n+1} = (r_k + r_n)/(s_k + s_n) where k is the greatest integer < n such that f_k <= x <= f_n. Sequence gives values s_n.
%C A399718 Write a 0 if f_n <= x and a 1 if f_n > x. This gives (for x = Pi/2) the sequence 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0 (31 times), 1, 0 (145 times), ... Ignore the initial 1, which is the term 1/0 and is the same for every x >= 1. Look at the run lengths of the remaining sequence, which are in this case L_1 = 2, L_2 = 1, L_3 = 1, L_4 = 3, L_5 = 31, L_6 = 1, L_7 = 145, etc., the continued fraction of Pi/2 (A053300). Christoffel showed that x has the continued fraction representation (L_1 - 1) + 1/(L_2 + 1/(L_3 + 1/(L_4 + ...))).
%D A399718 Edward J. Barbeau, Pell's Equation, New York: Springer-Verlag, 2003, p. 56, Exercise 1.4.
%D A399718 C. Brezinski, History of Continued Fractions and Padé Approximants, Springer-Verlag, 1991; pp. 151-152.
%D A399718 E. B. Christoffel, Observatio arithmetica, Ann. Math. Pura Appl., (II) 6 (1875), 148-153.
%H A399718 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A399718/b399718.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%F A399718 lim_{n->oo} A399717(n) / a(n) = Pi/2.
%e A399718 The fractions are 1/0, 0/1, 1/1, 2/1, 3/2, 5/3, 8/5, 11/7, 14/9, 25/16, 36/23, 47/30, ...
%t A399718 f[x_, n_] := (m = Floor[x]; f0 = {m, m + 1/2, m + 1};
%t A399718   r = ({a___, b_, c_, d___} /; b < x < c) :>
%t A399718       {b, (Numerator[b] + Numerator[c])/(Denominator[b] + Denominator[c]), c};
%t A399718   Join[{m, m + 1}, NestList[# /. r &, f0, n - 3][[All, 2]]]);
%t A399718 Join[{0, 1}, Denominator[f[Pi/2, 53]]]
%o A399718 (Python)
%o A399718 from sympy import Rational, pi
%o A399718 lo, hi = (0, 1), (1, 0)
%o A399718 A399718 = [0, 1]
%o A399718 for _ in range(53):
%o A399718     m = (lo[0] + hi[0], lo[1] + hi[1])
%o A399718     A399718.append(m[1])
%o A399718     if Rational(m[0], m[1]) <= pi/2:
%o A399718         lo = m
%o A399718     else:
%o A399718         hi = m
%o A399718 print(A399718)
%Y A399718 Cf. A019669, A053300, A063673, A097546, A399717 (numerators).
%K A399718 nonn,frac,new
%O A399718 0,5
